<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lykhny</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lykhny is a village in the Gudauta district of Abkhazia, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast. It was a residence of the Shervashidze princes of Abkhazia, whose palace ruins stand beside a 10th-century church with medieval wall paintings. Its meadow has long been a gathering place of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abkhaz-people">Abkhaz people</a>, and in 1989 the Lykhny Declaration demanding Abkhazia's separation from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> was adopted there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lykhny.jpg" alt="Lykhny" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vitosha · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lykhny_temple.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
